WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:06.628
Wow, wow, people.

00:00:06.628 --> 00:00:11.299
You know, actually, I love people
because my work is about people.

00:00:11.883 --> 00:00:14.386
It's actually about bringing
people together.

00:00:14.886 --> 00:00:16.054
I'm an artist.

00:00:16.054 --> 00:00:17.681
I mean, until I found a real job,

00:00:17.722 --> 00:00:20.100
but now it looks
like it's getting pretty serious.

00:00:20.141 --> 00:00:21.518
(Laughter)

00:00:21.518 --> 00:00:23.645
But, you know, I also love walls.

00:00:23.645 --> 00:00:27.023
And I know that walls
are supposed to divide us,

00:00:27.065 --> 00:00:29.568
but I think I've found a way

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:31.695
to use walls to bring us together.

00:00:32.237 --> 00:00:34.155
And I've tried this in different places.

00:00:34.197 --> 00:00:36.741
This was in Israel,
Palestine, 15 years ago,

00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:38.159
where, with my friend Marco,

00:00:38.159 --> 00:00:41.121
I took photos of Israeli
and Palestinian doing the same job

00:00:41.162 --> 00:00:43.999
and then pasted them
on Israeli city and Palestinian city,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.501
and people couldn't even
recognize who is who.

00:00:47.002 --> 00:00:49.296
This was in the favelas of Brazil.

00:00:49.838 --> 00:00:52.257
Faces of women on the hills
of the community.

00:00:52.299 --> 00:00:54.092
(Applause)

00:00:54.134 --> 00:00:57.304
Often the first target of the violence
that is happening there.

00:00:58.263 --> 00:01:01.308
This is a local museum we have in Paris,
it's called the Louvre.

00:01:01.349 --> 00:01:05.145
I don’t know if you’ve heard about it,
but I thought I’d give it a shout out.

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:07.897
And with 400 people and paper and glue,

00:01:07.897 --> 00:01:12.110
we wheat-pasted the entire plazas to make
the pyramid ten times bigger.

00:01:13.028 --> 00:01:15.905
This was at the border
between Mexico and US.

00:01:17.532 --> 00:01:18.700
Thank you.

00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:22.037
(Applause and cheers)

00:01:22.037 --> 00:01:24.539
Kikito, the little kid, is one year old,

00:01:24.581 --> 00:01:28.293
and he lives in the little house you see
on the top left of the image.

00:01:28.335 --> 00:01:32.922
I just wonder, at his age,
what is his perspective on the wall?

00:01:33.757 --> 00:01:38.261
You know, each time I do a project,
I wonder: Can art change the world?

00:01:39.346 --> 00:01:41.848
And I never really know
how a projects starts.

00:01:42.349 --> 00:01:44.684
Couple years ago, I was making breakfast

00:01:44.726 --> 00:01:46.311
and a friend of mine called me.

00:01:46.311 --> 00:01:49.898
Friend Saul, he says, "JR, you love walls.

00:01:49.898 --> 00:01:51.733
Why don't you do a project in prison?"

00:01:51.775 --> 00:01:55.445
I was like, you know, I was just,
"Dude, you know, I would do it,

00:01:55.487 --> 00:01:58.657
but it's too much paperwork
administrative, bureaucracy.

00:01:58.698 --> 00:02:01.910
Plus, I've been arrested 15 times,
they don't want me in there."

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.703
And he was like, "I know, bro,

00:02:03.745 --> 00:02:06.331
but you love walls so much,
what you would do in there.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.209
If you could do it, what could you do?"

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.127
I was like, "OK, you know what?"

00:02:12.253 --> 00:02:13.380
I had an idea.

00:02:13.421 --> 00:02:15.340
If I wanted to finish my poached eggs

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.260
and my, you know, French toast
before it gets cold,

00:02:19.302 --> 00:02:20.470
I had to get rid of him.

00:02:20.512 --> 00:02:23.390
So I told him, "You know what,
I'll paste the entire prison."

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.100
He was like, "Wow, that's amazing."

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.267
He hung up the phone,

00:02:26.309 --> 00:02:28.687
I thought I'd never hear
from him for two decades.

00:02:28.728 --> 00:02:31.272
He called his friend Scott,
who was like, "What's up?"

00:02:31.314 --> 00:02:34.484
Saul is like, “I spoke to JR,
he’s down to do a project in prison.”

00:02:34.484 --> 00:02:37.195
Scott's like, "That's amazing,
let me call the governor."

00:02:37.195 --> 00:02:39.072
Hung up the phone, called the governor.

00:02:39.114 --> 00:02:41.116
Governor was like, "Who's this JR?"

00:02:41.116 --> 00:02:42.534
"He does black and white,

00:02:42.575 --> 00:02:46.663
and, you know, he takes photos of people,
he records their story ..."

00:02:46.705 --> 00:02:48.456
"I'm sorry, I've never heard of it."

00:02:48.498 --> 00:02:50.250
"Yes, there's very large murals."

00:02:50.291 --> 00:02:53.044
And the governor was like,
"Wait, wait, wait.

00:02:53.086 --> 00:02:57.090
Before I was the governor,
I was in a mural.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.842
There were 1,300 people,

00:02:58.883 --> 00:03:01.761
each one of them recorded
their story, and I was one of them.

00:03:01.761 --> 00:03:03.430
Is that the same artist?"

00:03:03.471 --> 00:03:05.598
The guy said, "Yes,
that's the same artist."

00:03:05.598 --> 00:03:09.018
"Give him full clearance for every prison
in the state of California."

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.270
(Laughter and applause)

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:11.438
Calls me back.

00:03:11.479 --> 00:03:13.773
Next thing you know, I’m on Google Earth,

00:03:13.815 --> 00:03:14.983
and there's 35 prisons.

00:03:15.024 --> 00:03:18.611
I'm looking at them and I'm like,
well, first of all, I'm a wallpaper man.

00:03:18.611 --> 00:03:21.072
I cannot paste if I don't have
my clear surface.

00:03:21.072 --> 00:03:23.867
So that doesn't work,
that doesn't work, that doesn't work.

00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:26.327
The yard is made of sand and grass.

00:03:26.327 --> 00:03:27.495
I cannot do it.

00:03:27.537 --> 00:03:29.164
I know nothing about prisons.

00:03:29.205 --> 00:03:32.709
So then I see this one and I'm like,
"Wait, can we zoom in this one?"

00:03:32.751 --> 00:03:34.669
I'm like, "Oh, actually, you know what?

00:03:34.711 --> 00:03:37.213
The yard here in the center
looks like it's concrete.

00:03:37.255 --> 00:03:38.798
I could work on that."

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:42.385
"Look, JR, that's a supermax
security prison."

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:44.137
It's called Tehachapi,

00:03:44.179 --> 00:03:46.973
it's actually in the top five
of the most violent prisons.

00:03:46.973 --> 00:03:48.433
I was like, "That will do it."

00:03:48.433 --> 00:03:49.809
The next day, we flew there.

00:03:50.268 --> 00:03:51.728
We arrived there,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.481
and of course, you know,
it's not that easy to get there.

00:03:54.481 --> 00:03:56.524
It's like fences, electric fences, walls.

00:03:56.524 --> 00:03:59.402
And you add more walls
and more people that check your IDs.

00:03:59.402 --> 00:04:02.405
I get all the way to the yard.

00:04:02.447 --> 00:04:05.992
And it looked like some army guy
with bulletproof jackets and heavily armed

00:04:06.034 --> 00:04:08.453
who say, "Alright, this is going
to be very simple.

00:04:08.453 --> 00:04:10.872
There's some people
waiting for you in a gymnasium.

00:04:10.872 --> 00:04:12.123
We gathered some inmates.

00:04:12.123 --> 00:04:14.375
You cannot approach them,
you cannot touch them.

00:04:14.417 --> 00:04:16.795
You can sit at the chair
that we designed for you,

00:04:16.836 --> 00:04:19.422
and we're going to surround
the area of the gymnasium."

00:04:19.422 --> 00:04:20.715
That sounds fun.

00:04:21.174 --> 00:04:22.467
I enter the room,

00:04:22.467 --> 00:04:25.386
and we do this thing in France,
I don't know if you have that,

00:04:25.428 --> 00:04:27.263
but you shake people's hand, you know?

00:04:27.305 --> 00:04:30.725
So I started shaking people's hands,
"Hello, how are you? My name is JR.

00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:31.893
What's your name?"

00:04:31.935 --> 00:04:33.895
And go around the table, and then I sat.

00:04:33.895 --> 00:04:36.231
And I spoke with them.

00:04:36.606 --> 00:04:39.442
A lot of them have been there
since they were teenagers,

00:04:39.484 --> 00:04:42.195
some of them even from the age of 13.

00:04:42.654 --> 00:04:44.447
And I've never seen anything like it.

00:04:44.489 --> 00:04:47.242
And so I told them about my art
and about the idea.

00:04:47.283 --> 00:04:49.285
And they asked me a question, they said,

00:04:49.285 --> 00:04:51.830
"But what is the purpose of your art?"

00:04:52.288 --> 00:04:54.249
Well, you know, that's a good question.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:58.253
I don't know if I can answer before,
you know, trying a project.

00:04:58.294 --> 00:05:02.465
So I explained them an idea, and I said,
“Wait, before we start anything,

00:05:02.507 --> 00:05:04.175
I just want you to know,

00:05:04.175 --> 00:05:07.929
if there's anyone here in this room
that by being in this project,

00:05:07.929 --> 00:05:10.849
you might offend some
of your victims outside,

00:05:10.849 --> 00:05:12.016
I'm not your guy.

00:05:12.058 --> 00:05:14.310
I'm going to get
too much attention for you.

00:05:14.727 --> 00:05:17.772
If I were you, don't participate
in this project."

00:05:18.231 --> 00:05:20.942
Six or seven guys stood up
and left the room.

00:05:20.942 --> 00:05:22.110
I was like, "Alright."

00:05:22.151 --> 00:05:24.696
So we kept on going
and I started photographing them.

00:05:24.696 --> 00:05:27.490
Very simple, it's just a small
part of the process.

00:05:27.532 --> 00:05:28.992
I'm not really a photographer.

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:32.579
That’s just one part that I use,
and I get to meet them and talk with them.

00:05:32.579 --> 00:05:34.998
And each of them started
explaining me their story,

00:05:34.998 --> 00:05:36.124
where they grew up,

00:05:36.165 --> 00:05:37.333
where they come from,

00:05:37.333 --> 00:05:40.628
how many years, some of them
spent decades in this prison.

00:05:41.296 --> 00:05:43.423
And the thing is that,
I told them, I said,

00:05:43.464 --> 00:05:44.883
"Look, this photo is nothing.

00:05:44.883 --> 00:05:47.510
I need you to go in the next room.

00:05:47.552 --> 00:05:49.345
I'm going to leave a mic there.

00:05:49.387 --> 00:05:53.474
And I want you to record
your story from the beginning.

00:05:53.516 --> 00:05:54.642
I want to understand,

00:05:54.684 --> 00:05:57.729
you have to talk like,
if you're throwing a bottle in the ocean,

00:05:57.729 --> 00:05:59.898
I want to understand where you come from.

00:05:59.939 --> 00:06:03.526
And then what brought you to make
that crime that led you to prison.

00:06:04.444 --> 00:06:07.614
And if you changed,
explain how you changed and why."

00:06:07.655 --> 00:06:09.657
They all went, some stayed for 10 minutes,

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:11.409
some stayed for 20, 30 minutes.

00:06:11.409 --> 00:06:14.162
Some of them were crying in that place.

00:06:14.913 --> 00:06:17.373
Then I went back to my studio
and that's what we do.

00:06:17.373 --> 00:06:19.500
We print strips of paper
like a giant puzzle,

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.127
so we have to combine it together.

00:06:21.169 --> 00:06:23.671
It's all just paper and glue.

00:06:23.713 --> 00:06:25.965
And then we combine them by numbers,

00:06:26.007 --> 00:06:27.425
and then we went back there.

00:06:27.467 --> 00:06:30.345
And we started bringing
everybody in the yard.

00:06:30.845 --> 00:06:32.096
And we started pasting.

00:06:32.096 --> 00:06:35.391
Everyone from every gang,
every race, participated.

00:06:35.433 --> 00:06:36.893
Now, the thing is,

00:06:36.893 --> 00:06:39.103
we're still in a supermax security prison.

00:06:39.103 --> 00:06:40.313
So the guards were like,

00:06:40.313 --> 00:06:43.191
"Look, we love you
with your paper and stuff,

00:06:43.191 --> 00:06:45.610
but we’re going to count
everything you brought in,

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.404
and we’re going to make sure
you leave with everything out.

00:06:48.404 --> 00:06:50.865
So every hour we're going to stop
this whole project

00:06:50.865 --> 00:06:53.076
and make sure there's not
one scissors missing,

00:06:53.117 --> 00:06:56.079
one brush, one, you know,
bucket, anything."

00:06:56.496 --> 00:06:57.997
So we went through the process,

00:06:57.997 --> 00:07:01.334
and I had planned two to three days
to paste the whole thing.

00:07:01.334 --> 00:07:03.836
Those guys were so motivated,

00:07:03.836 --> 00:07:06.506
that in literally two
or three hours we had done it.

00:07:06.506 --> 00:07:07.924
So I said, "Wait, stop, guys,

00:07:07.966 --> 00:07:10.927
they're going to bring you
back to your cell, give me a second.

00:07:10.927 --> 00:07:13.471
I'm going to try to get
some guards to paste with us."

00:07:13.513 --> 00:07:16.182
And they were like, look,
"We love utopianism and stuff,

00:07:16.224 --> 00:07:17.642
but that’s where it stops.”

00:07:17.642 --> 00:07:19.018
"No, just give me a second."

00:07:19.018 --> 00:07:22.563
So I start going to the guards and I say,
"Hey, do you mind participating?"

00:07:22.605 --> 00:07:24.107
Fifty "no," one guy said "yes."

00:07:24.148 --> 00:07:25.650
I say, "Cool, come with me.

00:07:25.692 --> 00:07:27.151
They're going to show you."

00:07:27.193 --> 00:07:29.362
And then another guard and another guard.

00:07:29.404 --> 00:07:31.990
And that's where the real
walls were falling down,

00:07:32.031 --> 00:07:35.326
because there's no communication
between those guards and the inmates.

00:07:35.326 --> 00:07:39.414
(Applause)

00:07:39.455 --> 00:07:40.748
And you know what?

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:44.168
From the floor, it was so big
we couldn't see it anyway.

00:07:44.168 --> 00:07:45.586
You had to send a drone.

00:07:45.586 --> 00:07:48.589
So remember, again,
you're in a supermax security prison.

00:07:48.589 --> 00:07:49.882
It's geofencing.

00:07:49.924 --> 00:07:50.925
(Laughter)

00:07:50.925 --> 00:07:52.135
So we send in the drone,

00:07:52.176 --> 00:07:54.679
those guys have never seen
a drone their whole life,

00:07:54.679 --> 00:07:57.640
they were more excited about the drone
than the whole project.

00:07:57.682 --> 00:08:01.227
And I was so scared because I was like,
"I hope we did the puzzle right.

00:08:01.227 --> 00:08:04.147
Because if not, it's going to look
a bit ugly from up there."

00:08:04.147 --> 00:08:07.400
And that's what you see from up there.

00:08:07.442 --> 00:08:14.282
(Applause and cheers)

00:08:16.909 --> 00:08:18.077
Thank you.

00:08:18.119 --> 00:08:20.121
The whole yard, all their faces.

00:08:20.163 --> 00:08:23.458
And when you zoom in, actually,
you see the picnic table,

00:08:23.499 --> 00:08:26.961
you can still see, it looks like a hole,
but it's actually an illusion.

00:08:27.003 --> 00:08:28.880
And you see the guys walking on it.

00:08:29.338 --> 00:08:32.675
Now, the craziest part of all of it

00:08:32.675 --> 00:08:35.803
is because of that crazy
permit that I had,

00:08:35.803 --> 00:08:38.139
I literally walked in
without being searched.

00:08:38.139 --> 00:08:39.849
I had my phone on me.

00:08:39.849 --> 00:08:43.853
So I started filming in there
and posting it on social media.

00:08:44.187 --> 00:08:47.482
So this guy there was showing me
his tattoo on day one.

00:08:47.857 --> 00:08:50.568
And then the evening, he called
his family and they said,

00:08:50.568 --> 00:08:52.153
"We saw you on JR's Instagram.

00:08:52.195 --> 00:08:54.530
It's incredible, we see
what you guys are doing."

00:08:54.572 --> 00:08:56.365
He was so proud the next day he said,

00:08:56.407 --> 00:08:58.785
"JR, do you mind me showing my diplomas?"

00:08:58.785 --> 00:09:01.871
They started realizing the impact
of suddenly having a connection

00:09:01.871 --> 00:09:03.289
with the outside.

00:09:03.331 --> 00:09:04.874
Then this guy showed up.

00:09:04.874 --> 00:09:06.334
His name is Kevin.

00:09:06.751 --> 00:09:09.754
You know, when I saw him,
I was like, "Whoa."

00:09:09.796 --> 00:09:15.051
In my life, I'll never have a second
chance to ask a guy, hopefully,

00:09:15.051 --> 00:09:17.303
why he has a fucking swastika on his face.

00:09:17.345 --> 00:09:19.347
(Laughter)

00:09:19.388 --> 00:09:22.642
And so I ask him,
and he was like, "Oh, this?"

00:09:22.683 --> 00:09:24.185
Almost like he forgot about it.

00:09:24.185 --> 00:09:27.271
He was like, "I did this as a gang thing
when I went in prison.

00:09:27.271 --> 00:09:29.232
But now, if I could, I would remove it."

00:09:29.273 --> 00:09:32.235
I was like, "OK, do you mind
if I take a photo and I share it?"

00:09:32.276 --> 00:09:33.444
He's like, "Yeah, sure."

00:09:33.486 --> 00:09:34.654
I share that photo.

00:09:34.695 --> 00:09:36.656
Now, as you can imagine, on social media,

00:09:36.697 --> 00:09:39.951
a lot of people were as shocked
and offended as I was.

00:09:39.992 --> 00:09:42.245
But a lot of people were like,
“That’s strange,

00:09:42.245 --> 00:09:44.914
because that doesn't connect
with the beauty in his eyes

00:09:44.956 --> 00:09:46.749
and the humanity in him."

00:09:47.125 --> 00:09:49.418
So I went back to him
and I said, "Look, Kevin,

00:09:49.418 --> 00:09:51.337
there's this thing called social media.

00:09:51.337 --> 00:09:54.298
I know you've been here too long
and you don't know about this,

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.301
but people are writing comments,
I'm going to read them to you.

00:09:57.343 --> 00:09:59.345
And I think some of them
you should answer.

00:09:59.387 --> 00:10:02.098
So we started talking,
and I did many and many videos

00:10:02.098 --> 00:10:04.392
and asking him again and again,
and he responded

00:10:04.433 --> 00:10:06.686
and he went deeper and deeper.

00:10:06.727 --> 00:10:08.938
And it started a chain
of people who were like,

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.232
"Let's get this tattoo out of his face."

00:10:11.274 --> 00:10:15.361
So now before we revealed
the image that you saw,

00:10:15.361 --> 00:10:17.697
I didn't want to throw it
to the world like that.

00:10:17.697 --> 00:10:19.991
For now, only the inmates
and I have seen it.

00:10:20.032 --> 00:10:22.493
We started an app
where you can actually go in,

00:10:22.535 --> 00:10:23.870
it's totally free,

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:26.956
and you can go and click
on any face and hear their story

00:10:26.998 --> 00:10:28.332
for as much as they want.

00:10:28.749 --> 00:10:35.006
(Applause)

00:10:35.006 --> 00:10:37.008
I wonder, you know, I've seen it.

00:10:37.049 --> 00:10:41.137
I saw people telling me that they
heard story, and like a podcast.

00:10:41.179 --> 00:10:43.514
But I wonder, how would
they feel about it?

00:10:43.514 --> 00:10:45.600
You know, I wanted the feedback from them.

00:10:45.641 --> 00:10:48.060
So I got a permit to go back into prison.

00:10:48.102 --> 00:10:51.522
And this time I said, let me work
on some more walls, I'll find an idea.

00:10:52.273 --> 00:10:55.359
So I go back in there
and I started working on the walls,

00:10:55.401 --> 00:10:58.613
but it was really, the excuse was like,
I need to speak to you guys.

00:10:58.654 --> 00:11:00.281
So I said, "What happened, guys?"

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:02.909
They were like, "Well, what do
you mean, what happened?"

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:05.995
I was like, "I was on the outside,
I couldn't speak to you guys.

00:11:05.995 --> 00:11:07.413
What was the impact inside?"

00:11:07.413 --> 00:11:09.332
"Oh, the impact inside is pretty simple.

00:11:09.373 --> 00:11:13.211
Let me tell you for myself, I mean,
my daughter never visited me in 14 years.

00:11:13.669 --> 00:11:17.048
And now she sat in her bedroom,
and she listened to my audio,

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:19.008
and now she sees me every week.”

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:21.969
He says, "You can ask whoever
you want of those guys here,

00:11:21.969 --> 00:11:23.763
and they'll tell you the same story."

00:11:23.804 --> 00:11:26.557
(Applause)

00:11:26.599 --> 00:11:29.018
And he told me another thing happened.

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:32.188
"Is that the guards
started listening to our stories.

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:33.898
They treat us differently now."

00:11:35.983 --> 00:11:38.736
Then we started seeing
the walls falling down.

00:11:38.736 --> 00:11:40.863
So I left a little souvenir
in the courtyard.

00:11:40.905 --> 00:11:43.866
I was like, "Let me make one more
wall disappear before I go."

00:11:43.908 --> 00:11:48.246
So we pasted the mountains
from behind the wall on the wall.

00:11:48.287 --> 00:11:49.830
And it's still there, actually.

00:11:49.872 --> 00:11:55.044
(Applause)

00:11:55.086 --> 00:11:58.464
This one is still in level four,
which is the supermax security prison.

00:11:58.464 --> 00:12:01.175
And they told me in winter
it becomes all snowy.

00:12:01.217 --> 00:12:03.344
And so you actually
don't tell the difference

00:12:03.386 --> 00:12:05.304
between the reality and the wall.

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:07.723
Now, Kevin was still in prison,

00:12:07.765 --> 00:12:09.475
so I visited him again.

00:12:09.976 --> 00:12:12.728
And something had changed in him.

00:12:13.229 --> 00:12:14.730
He was already helping others,

00:12:14.772 --> 00:12:18.484
and, you know, you could tell that he
almost had forgotten about his tattoo,

00:12:18.484 --> 00:12:19.777
even if it was still there,

00:12:19.819 --> 00:12:22.280
because you cannot remove
tattoos in prison.

00:12:22.321 --> 00:12:24.407
So I bought him a book,

00:12:24.407 --> 00:12:27.159
and I told him, "Look,
there's a swastika on the cover,

00:12:27.201 --> 00:12:28.619
I thought you would like it."

00:12:28.619 --> 00:12:29.787
And he laughed.

00:12:29.787 --> 00:12:32.039
But I said, "No, seriously,
this is our friend,

00:12:32.039 --> 00:12:33.624
Art Spiegelman, who wrote it,

00:12:33.624 --> 00:12:36.502
and he told the story
about his family in the Holocaust."

00:12:37.003 --> 00:12:39.839
And so that night he went
and read the book.

00:12:40.881 --> 00:12:42.383
And he was really moved.

00:12:42.425 --> 00:12:43.884
He called his mom and he said,

00:12:43.884 --> 00:12:47.930
"Mom, those French people brought me
this book that talks about the Holocaust."

00:12:47.972 --> 00:12:50.224
And she said, "But you stupid moron.

00:12:50.224 --> 00:12:51.600
Your family was from Poland.

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:52.810
They were hiding Jews.

00:12:52.852 --> 00:12:56.063
They died in Auschwitz
because of protecting them.

00:12:56.105 --> 00:12:58.441
And you go in prison
and do this on your face?"

00:12:58.482 --> 00:12:59.817
He was in shock.

00:13:01.652 --> 00:13:05.031
Couple years later,
which is a couple months ago,

00:13:05.031 --> 00:13:06.198
he came out.

00:13:06.824 --> 00:13:09.618
Like many others from the project,

00:13:09.618 --> 00:13:13.748
almost all of them got moved
to a lower-security prison,

00:13:13.748 --> 00:13:18.336
and one third of them got freed
because of having good grades

00:13:18.377 --> 00:13:20.880
and notes by the guards
after the project.

00:13:20.921 --> 00:13:24.091
So Kevin went out,
and the first thing he did

00:13:24.091 --> 00:13:27.511
was to go up that hill
that he looked at for 17 years,

00:13:27.553 --> 00:13:30.389
to look down at the prison
that he was staying in.

00:13:30.431 --> 00:13:33.392
And then the second thing,
as I promised him,

00:13:33.434 --> 00:13:36.520
I took him to a doctor
to remove his tattoo.

00:13:37.146 --> 00:13:40.858
And the session started with the laser,
and it's very painful.

00:13:41.942 --> 00:13:46.697
And at the end of it, the doctor removed
the laser and she told him,

00:13:46.739 --> 00:13:49.992
"Well, who's better than a Jewish doctor
to remove your swastika?"

00:13:49.992 --> 00:13:56.665
(Laughter and applause)

00:13:56.707 --> 00:13:57.875
And that's him now.

00:13:57.875 --> 00:14:04.840
(Applause and cheers)

00:14:04.965 --> 00:14:08.594
You know, we really tried
to bring him here.

00:14:08.844 --> 00:14:11.305
But his parole officer,
who is actually really nice,

00:14:11.305 --> 00:14:13.099
he said "JR, you're pushing too much.

00:14:13.099 --> 00:14:14.517
He's not going to Canada.

00:14:14.558 --> 00:14:15.726
Sorry about this.”

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:18.437
But he knows I'm here talking about it.

00:14:18.437 --> 00:14:21.065
You know, I want to use art as a bridge

00:14:21.065 --> 00:14:22.983
to make people talk to each other.

00:14:23.317 --> 00:14:25.277
I'm not an activist, I'm just an artist.

00:14:25.277 --> 00:14:27.405
I don't try to tell people what to think.

00:14:27.446 --> 00:14:29.407
I just try to make them think.

00:14:29.407 --> 00:14:31.242
And I really see art,

00:14:31.283 --> 00:14:33.619
to me, it's like, it's in the process.

00:14:33.661 --> 00:14:34.912
That's what's important.

00:14:34.912 --> 00:14:38.541
And after this whole project,
after everything I showed you,

00:14:38.541 --> 00:14:41.377
what shook them,
what struck them the most,

00:14:41.377 --> 00:14:43.129
is that I shook their hand.

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:45.381
So at the end, they even
asked me for a hug.

00:14:47.258 --> 00:14:50.719
Now I remember
their first question, which was:

00:14:50.719 --> 00:14:52.596
What is the purpose of your art?

00:14:53.389 --> 00:14:57.226
Well, art can change things,
but can it change the world?

00:14:57.268 --> 00:14:59.061
Or can it change a man?

00:14:59.603 --> 00:15:01.147
Before you answer that question,

00:15:01.188 --> 00:15:05.109
think, at some point in your life,
have you changed?

00:15:05.734 --> 00:15:08.112
And if yes, if you did,

00:15:08.154 --> 00:15:09.572
why can't they?

00:15:09.613 --> 00:15:10.781
Thank you.

00:15:13.159 --> 00:15:20.082
(Applause and cheers)

00:15:26.088 --> 00:15:28.299
Helen Walters: That's amazing,
you're amazing.

00:15:29.467 --> 00:15:32.178
You also just got back from Ukraine,

00:15:32.178 --> 00:15:35.097
and I wanted to show us
just another piece of your work.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.392
JR: Oh yeah, that was in Ukraine.

00:15:38.893 --> 00:15:41.812
The biggest city in the west side
of Ukraine is called Lviv.

00:15:42.229 --> 00:15:46.233
And a friend of mine
took that photo at the border,

00:15:46.275 --> 00:15:49.111
he sent it to me,
I printed it 150 feet long.

00:15:49.111 --> 00:15:53.032
We rolled it as a tarp,
walked through the border with it.

00:15:53.032 --> 00:15:55.951
It's actually easy to go that way,
you know, to enter Ukraine.

00:15:55.993 --> 00:15:58.871
So they were like, "Oh, you're
going to go this way? Sure."

00:15:58.913 --> 00:16:02.750
And then we drove to, you know,
I met some people on Instagram,

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:04.919
and they came
and picked me up in their car.

00:16:05.461 --> 00:16:08.172
And then we gathered hundreds of people.

00:16:08.172 --> 00:16:11.675
We wanted to show Putin's planes
who they were shooting out.

00:16:12.259 --> 00:16:14.762
(Applause)

00:16:14.762 --> 00:16:17.264
That little girl is actually safe.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:20.893
When she got photographed,
she was coming out of the country.

00:16:20.893 --> 00:16:24.355
So she’s now in Warsaw, and she’s OK.

00:16:24.396 --> 00:16:26.190
And since then, actually,

00:16:26.232 --> 00:16:28.234
we're moving this image all around Europe.

00:16:28.275 --> 00:16:31.111
So while TED was happening
in the last four days,

00:16:31.111 --> 00:16:34.782
the image was in Berlin,
Dusseldorf, Venice this morning.

00:16:34.823 --> 00:16:37.660
And then it will keep traveling
and each time, each place,

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:40.538
people are gathering by themselves
and opening it up.

00:16:40.579 --> 00:16:42.414
HW: That is amazing, JR, thank you.

00:16:42.414 --> 00:16:43.582
JR: Thank you.

00:16:43.582 --> 00:16:47.419
(Applause)

